There’s a hard truth in the first half: nobody has direct access to another person. We love through a model.

What someone experiences as “you” is filtered through memory, attachment, expectation, attraction, fear, prior relationships, culture, and whatever state they are in when they encounter you. In that sense:

\text{person} \rightarrow \text{their model of you} \rightarrow \text{their experience of you}

So yes, part of love is always love of a representation.

But I wouldn’t go all the way to “therefore nobody loves you.” Mature love is partly the opposite process: continually allowing the real person to correct the projection. You discover things about them that violate your fantasy, and instead of forcing them back into the fantasy, you update.

That gives a useful distinction:

\text{infatuation} \approx \text{love the projection}

\text{mature love} \approx \text{keep revising the projection toward the person}

You can never remove mediation completely, but you can become less possessive of your model.

The “how you make me feel” part is also real but incomplete. Relationships necessarily involve reinforcement. People are drawn toward those who make them feel safe, desired, understood, alive, respected, amused, challenged, etc. But love can extend beyond immediate reward. Caring for someone through illness, failure, depression, aging, disability, conflict, or periods when they are giving you almost nothing pleasurable is evidence that attachment can exceed simple hedonic exchange.

And unconditional love is probably taught too simplistically. There is an important distinction between:

unconditional worth: “Your value as a person does not disappear when you fail.”

and

unconditional relationship: “Nothing you do can cause me to leave, withdraw trust, or impose boundaries.”

The second is neither realistic nor necessarily healthy.

Children often receive something closer to unconditional care because they are dependent and parents accept asymmetric responsibility for them. Romantic relationships are fundamentally different. Adults choose each other, so reciprocity, trust, attraction, compatibility, and conduct inevitably matter.

I also understand the male piece you’re pointing at. Men can receive a lot of messaging that feels like:

be useful, competent, strong, providing, composed, desirable—and then you will be valued.

That can make affection feel contingent on performance. But women experience their own conditionality around beauty, youth, sexuality, caregiving, agreeableness, fertility, status, etc. The currencies differ more than the underlying human problem does.

The deeper wound is probably:

“Would you still choose me if I stopped producing the thing you currently value me for?”

That question sits underneath a lot of adult love.

Maybe the closest humans get to something genuinely profound isn’t “I love you without any conditions whatsoever.”

It is:

“I know the person in my head is not completely you. Keep showing me who you are, and I will keep trying to meet the real person rather than demanding that you remain my projection.”

That is less absolute than unconditional love, but I think it is more real.